							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg28519#msg28519">Quote from: Geo on May 24, 2007, 08:37:52 pm</a></div><div class="quote"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg28218#msg28218">Quote from: Blind Sage on May 22, 2007, 08:23:04 pm</a></div><div class="quote">The people of Tibet sure seem to appreciate it.<br /></div><br />You&#039;re right. Alot of them do:<br /><a href="http&#58;//www.michaelparenti.org/Tibet.html" target="_blank">http://www.michaelparenti.org/Tibet.html</a><br /></div><br />I agree with Budaho, very nice link, and very insightful.&nbsp; I appreciate the knowledge.&nbsp; Thanks Geo.<br /><br />But here&#039;s my ongoing question, if the solution is Marxism and Mao&#039;s version is so much better than everything out there, and apparently seems to have no flaws whenever someone criticizes it, then why are there still serious problems and complaints against the Chinese regime by the Tibetans, and why is it okay that for a Marxist regime to enter a region, overthrow or or at least subsume the government of that region, interfere and try to change its traditions -maybe, maybe not for the better- but we don&#039;t call it colonialism, imperialism, or immoral, and they aren&#039;t held to the same critical standards as a capitalist regime.&nbsp; Is it justifiable because the basic foundation of Marxism is theoretically of a higher moral calibre than other systems and therefore is more okay to impose on others than a capitalist one?<br /><br /></div>
