							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg28667#msg28667">Quote from: Blind Sage on May 26, 2007, 11:27:03 am</a></div><div class="quote">Your preaching to the choir Budaho.  I agree with everything you said.  My issue is that people on here seem to be extremely critical of everything to do with capitalism, but don&#039;t turn that same level of criticism back on Marxist/communist/socialist theory and practice.  Red Star just got done defending Mao from any and all criticism even though that criticism is both documentable and illustrated by Flowside&#039;s personal family experience.  When Geo says &quot;I&#039;ve experienced the effects of capital first hand&quot; (I may be paraphrasing that) and people cheer, then Flowside basically says &quot;My family&#039;s experienced the effects of Maoism first hand&quot; and the same people become real defensive, it doesn&#039;t add up.  If you&#039;re searching for answers and truth, then the critical eye that you aim in one direction should be aimed in all directions equally.<br /></div><br />yeah i know what you mean.&nbsp; i know i&#039;m guilty of that alot.&nbsp; i don&#039;t believe that there is a difference between attrocious actions committed by one side or the other, but there is a deffinate difference in the amount of criticism i direct either way.&nbsp; i&#039;m not really sure why, but in part its that&nbsp; in society at large there&#039;s already so much criticism out there directed towards the communist and socialist regimes.&nbsp; its the internal criticism that seems to be lacking, so thats where i direct most of my energy, but there&#039;s more to it than that.&nbsp; <br /><br />along the same lines as this i wonder why people in all sorts of different antiestablishment kinds of communities are quick to bring up all the extenuating circumstances that forced whoever to do whatever horrible thing in some communist country, but willfully ignore that there are also extenuating circumstances behind the bad things done by america and capitalism in its history.&nbsp; too often people, myself included, jump to blame greed or just plain evillness for the wrongs capitalism is responsible for.&nbsp; this kind of response is easy, and seems like the instinctive assumption to make, but it does nothing to further a solution to the problems.&nbsp; a solution must address the systemic flaws in our economic social and political structures, blaming everything on greedy CEOs only distracts people&#039;s attention from these more important issues (which is why communist ideology is so often portrayed in that way in the media).&nbsp; </div>
						</td>
